# Origin:  none -- Veil-authored, and a correction rather than a new surface. clearcotelabs/
#          clearcote-browser's `030-hardware-concurrency` was audited as the counterpart
#          (Veil/docs/MIGRATION-UNGOOGLED.md 6.5, verdict `delta`) and is NOT the source: its
#          `deviceMemory` body is a three-rung `flag > ungoogled::CurrentPersona() > real` ladder
#          whose middle rung is the second identity source 6.9 rejected, and both of its outer rungs
#          clamp to 8 with the comment "real Chrome never reports >8". That is false in Chromium 151
#          and measurably so -- see below. What is taken is the argument that the field needs a
#          switch at all.
# Source:  n/a -- authored in this repository.
# Commit:  n/a -- see this repository's git log for this file.
# License: BSD-3-Clause (see CREDITS.md), same terms as the code it edits.
# Changes: n/a -- not a port.
#
# `005` replaced `NavigatorDeviceMemory::deviceMemory()`'s body with `return 8;`, gated on nothing --
# not a switch, not the seed, not the platform (measured 5.1e). Two consequences, and the second is
# the one that makes this a correction rather than a feature: every Veil profile reports the same 8,
# which is a fleet-wide constant a detector can key on; and the `deviceMemory` field the UI stores
# reaches nothing, so `fingerprintFieldState()` has had to call it *spoofed but not configurable*
# since Stage 5.4. `905` gives it the switch, which moves it to *settable*.
#
# TWO RUNGS, THE SAME TWO `005` ALREADY GIVES ITS OTHER FIELD. `--fingerprint-device-memory=<n>`
# first, then `ApproximatedDeviceMemory::GetApproximatedDeviceMemory()` -- the host's real value,
# which is what `005` falls back to for `hardwareConcurrency` in the file next door. One rule for the
# two fields of one block, and "unset" means the truth rather than a constant. No seed gate: a
# declared RAM size is not a cross-platform substitution, so gating it on `--fingerprint` would
# disable the field in exactly the case it is clearest.
#
# THE REACHABLE SET IS FIVE VALUES, AND THE COUNTERPART'S CLAMP IS AGAINST A STALE SPEC.
# `approximated_device_memory.cc:33-79` rounds physical memory to the nearer neighbouring power of
# two, divides by 1024, and clamps to `kMinMemory = 2.0f` / `kMaxMemory = 32.0f` on desktop -- 1 and 8
# on Android -- citing crbug.com/454354290 for the update. So a desktop Chrome 151 can report exactly
# 2, 4, 8, 16 or 32, and **stock Chrome 151 on this 32 GiB host reports 32** (measured 2026-08-28,
# Veil/docs/MIGRATION-UNGOOGLED.md 6.18). clearcote's `> 8 -> 8` clamp therefore does not spec-cap
# anything; it replaces a true value with a smaller one and calls the result the spec.
#
# A value outside the five **falls through to the host** rather than being clamped into it. Clamping
# turns a typo into a different machine silently; falling through leaves the real value, which is the
# direction `906` and `963` both take. Veil's `validate` refuses anything but the five, so a command
# line carrying a sixth is hand-written and the engine's only duty is to not report an impossible
# number.
#
# The switch **is** in `PropagateBrowserCommandLineToRenderer`'s allow-list, because
# `NavigatorDeviceMemory` is Blink and a renderer has to see it. `961`, landing in the same phase, is
# deliberately absent from that list for the mirror-image reason: the browser process is its only
# reader. A switch is propagated when a process that reads it needs it, and not otherwise (6.9).
#
# SERIES POSITION. `905` corrects `005` and so must apply after it, but it is placed at the end of
# `patches/series` rather than immediately after `005` -- the position `906` and `915` hold relative
# to their targets. The reason is mechanical and worth stating rather than leaving as a puzzle: `961`
# and `905` both append a constant to the tail of `components/ungoogled/ungoogled_switches.{h,cc}`,
# so whichever applies second has to carry the first one's line in its context. `patches/series` is a
# dependency order, not a numeric one -- `950` already sits before `012` in it for the same class of
# reason.
#
# Veil-authored `9NN` numbering: `905` corrects the `005` of the same number, which is the convention
# `906` and `915` follow.

